1
00:00:00,352 --> 00:00:03,798
Now, for this section here, I prepared a

2
00:00:03,862 --> 00:00:04,583
basic

3
00:00:05,144 --> 00:00:06,971
demo application, a dummy

4
00:00:07,548 --> 00:00:10,160
app which we'll use to dive into those

5
00:00:10,448 --> 00:00:11,618
essential features.

6
00:00:12,516 --> 00:00:15,384
And it's a Node.js application, specifically

7
00:00:15,497 --> 00:00:17,436
a Node.js Express

8
00:00:18,045 --> 00:00:20,961
application. So an application that uses the

9
00:00:20,961 --> 00:00:23,766
Node.js Express framework.

10
00:00:24,615 --> 00:00:24,888
And

11
00:00:25,497 --> 00:00:28,317
you might be wondering, why is that the

12
00:00:28,317 --> 00:00:30,689
case? Isn't this course about Angular?

13
00:00:32,629 --> 00:00:34,055
Well, yes, it is,

14
00:00:34,809 --> 00:00:39,778
but HTMX is a library that aims to simplify

15
00:00:39,778 --> 00:00:41,189
the process of sending

16
00:00:41,766 --> 00:00:44,155
AJAX requests mostly.

17
00:00:45,085 --> 00:00:48,467
So that aims to simplify the process of

18
00:00:48,515 --> 00:00:51,753
sending behind-the-scenes requests from

19
00:00:51,962 --> 00:00:52,892
inside a website

20
00:00:53,549 --> 00:00:56,338
and of handling the responses to those

21
00:00:56,370 --> 00:00:56,947
requests.

22
00:00:58,359 --> 00:01:00,188
So therefore, in order to

23
00:01:00,766 --> 00:01:04,375
use HTMX and in order for me to show you the

24
00:01:04,375 --> 00:01:07,199
core features that make up HTMX,

25
00:01:07,841 --> 00:01:11,515
we need a backend server. We need a server

26
00:01:11,515 --> 00:01:14,788
to which those requests can be sent, and

27
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:17,627
that will be this Node.js Express server.

28
00:01:18,109 --> 00:01:22,553
Now, I also use this server to then render

29
00:01:22,585 --> 00:01:25,136
the page which we'll see on the screen and

30
00:01:25,328 --> 00:01:27,975
where we'll use HTMX though.

31
00:01:28,937 --> 00:01:33,090
Because whilst that could be a decoupled web

32
00:01:33,090 --> 00:01:35,929
application, a decoupled frontend,

33
00:01:36,538 --> 00:01:41,205
it's quite common when working with HTMX

34
00:01:41,766 --> 00:01:44,556
to use it in the context of a full-stack

35
00:01:44,717 --> 00:01:48,197
application where you as a developer work on

36
00:01:48,421 --> 00:01:52,125
both the backend and the frontend and where

37
00:01:52,334 --> 00:01:56,311
HTMX is used on pages that are rendered by

38
00:01:56,439 --> 00:01:59,726
the backend. Because as you'll also see

39
00:01:59,726 --> 00:02:02,002
throughout this section and this course,

40
00:02:02,852 --> 00:02:06,379
those requests which HTMX will send to the

41
00:02:06,379 --> 00:02:06,956
backend

42
00:02:07,485 --> 00:02:11,909
actually require responses that send back

43
00:02:11,909 --> 00:02:15,885
HTML code. So you'll need to make sure that

44
00:02:15,901 --> 00:02:19,075
your backend does that anyways, and that's

45
00:02:19,428 --> 00:02:21,576
why it is quite common to have one

46
00:02:21,848 --> 00:02:23,339
integrated application.

47
00:02:24,719 --> 00:02:26,951
And that is exactly what we have here in

48
00:02:27,063 --> 00:02:29,696
this starting project. It's a very simple

49
00:02:29,728 --> 00:02:33,532
Node.js application which offers a GET route

50
00:02:33,645 --> 00:02:36,213
to which we can send requests where I send

51
00:02:36,294 --> 00:02:37,465
back a document,

52
00:02:38,445 --> 00:02:41,527
a document where I have some dummy content.

53
00:02:42,378 --> 00:02:44,930
And that's all I have for now. And we'll add

54
00:02:45,026 --> 00:02:47,739
more to this app. And most importantly,

55
00:02:47,852 --> 00:02:50,484
we'll add a lot of HTMX code to this app

56
00:02:50,790 --> 00:02:51,913
throughout this section.

57
00:02:52,828 --> 00:02:56,007
Now, what's worth noting is that here in

58
00:02:56,184 --> 00:02:59,363
this example project, and actually in all

59
00:02:59,796 --> 00:03:01,369
the example projects I'll

60
00:03:02,060 --> 00:03:03,457
give you throughout this course,

61
00:03:04,291 --> 00:03:06,603
I put this HTML code

62
00:03:07,261 --> 00:03:09,589
into a string and I'm sending back that

63
00:03:09,589 --> 00:03:12,447
string which contains the HTML code as a

64
00:03:12,495 --> 00:03:14,791
response from that server to the frontend.

65
00:03:15,722 --> 00:03:16,718
Of course, in

66
00:03:17,312 --> 00:03:21,328
bigger Node.js projects, you might instead

67
00:03:21,328 --> 00:03:24,445
use a template engine like EJS or

68
00:03:24,445 --> 00:03:25,232
Handlebars.

69
00:03:25,826 --> 00:03:28,204
And I just wanna emphasize that you would of

70
00:03:28,236 --> 00:03:32,221
course use HTMX in exactly the same way as

71
00:03:32,317 --> 00:03:35,241
you'll learn it in this course if you were

72
00:03:35,530 --> 00:03:36,896
using such a setup.

73
00:03:37,538 --> 00:03:41,555
I just use this string-based solution so

74
00:03:41,555 --> 00:03:44,045
that all the code is essentially in one

75
00:03:44,045 --> 00:03:46,530
place and we don't have to deal with a lot

76
00:03:46,530 --> 00:03:48,438
of subfolders and files.

77
00:03:49,176 --> 00:03:51,260
But in the end, the result will be the same.

78
00:03:51,613 --> 00:03:55,333
Some HTML code, some HTML document, you

79
00:03:55,365 --> 00:03:55,717
could say,

80
00:03:56,391 --> 00:03:59,373
is sent back from the server to the client,

81
00:03:59,453 --> 00:04:00,672
to the browser therefore.

82
00:04:01,201 --> 00:04:05,754
And that HTML code can contain HTMX and can

83
00:04:05,851 --> 00:04:10,484
use HTMX, no matter how that HTML code is

84
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:12,841
generated or stored on the server.

85
00:04:13,546 --> 00:04:15,549
Now, in order to use this attached project,

86
00:04:15,710 --> 00:04:17,697
you should therefore download it and then

87
00:04:17,793 --> 00:04:20,677
run npm install to make sure that all the

88
00:04:20,677 --> 00:04:22,568
dependencies that are required are

89
00:04:22,697 --> 00:04:23,273
installed.

90
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:28,049
And then you can start this Node.js server

91
00:04:28,065 --> 00:04:29,555
by running npm start.

92
00:04:30,773 --> 00:04:33,273
And this will start in watch mode so that

93
00:04:33,273 --> 00:04:35,308
whenever you make changes to the Node.js

94
00:04:35,437 --> 00:04:37,680
code and you save them and you then reload

95
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:38,145
the page,

96
00:04:38,722 --> 00:04:41,018
those changes are reflected instantly

97
00:04:41,275 --> 00:04:43,620
without you needing to restart the server.

98
00:04:44,985 --> 00:04:46,848
And you should of course keep that server up

99
00:04:46,848 --> 00:04:48,807
and running as long as you are working on

100
00:04:48,839 --> 00:04:52,517
the project so that you can send requests to

101
00:04:52,533 --> 00:04:52,742
it.

102
00:04:53,737 --> 00:04:57,880
With it started, it's then localhost:3000 to

103
00:04:57,897 --> 00:04:59,984
which you can send a request in your browser

104
00:05:00,257 --> 00:05:03,116
to load this dummy starting website. And

105
00:05:03,132 --> 00:05:05,476
that's now where we'll get started with

106
00:05:05,541 --> 00:05:06,392
HTMX.
